<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>Label</key>
	<string>com.atlas20.daily-refresh</string>
	<!--
	  Refreshes the raw provider cache and rebuilds the processed panel.

	  Why a wrapper script instead of a single command: the download step only
	  updates data/raw, while every strategy reads data/processed/panel_daily.csv.
	  Running both under one lock keeps "new data arrived" and "the panel knows
	  about it" in the same job, so a silent half-refresh cannot leave research
	  running on a stale panel - and a slow run can never race a catch-up fire
	  into the same raw cache.

	  The schedule fires five times, and scripts/refresh_daily_data.py exits
	  immediately when the panel already carries the last completed UTC day, so
	  only the runs that are actually needed do any work.  The first pair is the
	  fast path; the old pair and the late run remain fail-safe fallbacks:

	    08:30 Beijing (00:30 UTC)  early primary, after the 10-asset probe's
	                               measured 00:20 CMC publication window
	    08:50 Beijing (00:50 UTC)  early catch-up, before the 09:05 trade
	    10:30 Beijing (02:30 UTC)  legacy primary fallback
	    10:55 Beijing (02:55 UTC)  legacy pre-trade fallback before 11:05
	    14:30 Beijing (06:30 UTC)  late catch-up for anything that still failed

	  The pre-trade catch-up is what makes a shutdown in the middle of the
	  primary run survivable: on 2026-10-16 the machine went down at 10:34 and
	  killed the primary mid-write, so the panel stayed on 10-14 and the 11:05
	  trade job failed closed.  With this fire the panel is repaired 10 minutes
	  before the trade, and the trade job can also repair it itself.

	  Both the download and the build write through temp files, and the lock is
	  released by the OS if the process is killed, so a reboot cannot leave a
	  stale lock behind.

	  launchd reads StartCalendarInterval in the machine's LOCAL time zone,
	  not UTC. This workstation runs Asia/Shanghai (UTC+8), so the UTC times
	  above map to the Hour/Minute pairs below. On a machine in another zone,
	  convert again. tests/test_launchd_refresh_schedule.py pins this.
	-->
	<key>ProgramArguments</key>
	<array>
		<string>/bin/zsh</string>
		<string>-lc</string>
		<string>cd /Users/ww/Project/Atlas20 &amp;&amp; .venv/bin/python scripts/refresh_daily_data.py --config config/base.yaml</string>
	</array>
	<key>WorkingDirectory</key>
	<string>/Users/ww/Project/Atlas20</string>
	<key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>Hour</key>
			<integer>8</integer>
			<key>Minute</key>
			<integer>30</integer>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>Hour</key>
			<integer>8</integer>
			<key>Minute</key>
			<integer>50</integer>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>Hour</key>
			<integer>10</integer>
			<key>Minute</key>
			<integer>30</integer>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>Hour</key>
			<integer>10</integer>
			<key>Minute</key>
			<integer>55</integer>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>Hour</key>
			<integer>14</integer>
			<key>Minute</key>
			<integer>30</integer>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>RunAtLoad</key>
	<false/>
	<key>StandardOutPath</key>
	<string>/Users/ww/Library/Logs/atlas20-daily-refresh.log</string>
	<key>StandardErrorPath</key>
	<string>/Users/ww/Library/Logs/atlas20-daily-refresh.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
